---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="organising">
  <h2 id="collections">Collections and libraries</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>collection</strong> is one folder of notes. You can have several open at once — work,
    personal, a project — each with a row of its own in the sidebar, and the set of open
    collections is a <strong>library</strong> you can save and reopen from{' '}
    <strong>File ▸ Open…</strong> (<kbd>⌘O</kbd>). Recently opened collections are listed there
    too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Collections are opened, created and closed from the sidebar: the <strong>+</strong> in its
    header has <strong>New Folder…</strong>, <strong>New Collection</strong>,{' '}
    <strong>Open Collection</strong>, <strong>Open Recent…</strong> and{' '}
    <strong>Open Default Collection</strong>, and a collection’s own <strong>…</strong> menu has{' '}
    <strong>Close Collection</strong>. Closing one removes it from the sidebar and deletes nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each collection keeps its own folder tree, tags and bookmarks. Search spans all of them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="search">Search</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Search Notes</strong>, under the magnifying glass in the bar over the note — or{' '}
    <kbd>⌥⌘F</kbd> — opens a search field in the bar. It searches titles first (instantly, from the
    index) and then, from the third character you type, note contents, showing a snippet of the
    matching text. The results take the
    tree’s place in the sidebar, grouped by collection, and matching attachments such as PDFs
    appear alongside notes. Clear the field and the tree comes back.
  </p>
  <p>
    On iPhone, the <strong>Search</strong> tab searches the collection you are in. To find and
    replace inside one note — with regular expressions if you like — see{' '}
    <a href={href('/manual/editor')}>The editor</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-quickly">Open Quickly</h2>
  <p>
    <kbd>⇧⌘O</kbd> — or <strong>Open Quickly…</strong> under the same magnifying glass — opens a
    fuzzy finder over note titles, aliases and headings. Type a few letters, press <kbd>↩</kbd>. It
    is the fastest way to move around a large collection without touching the mouse.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tags">Tags</h2>
  <p>
    Write <code>#tag</code> anywhere in a note. Typing <code>#</code> offers autocomplete from tags
    already in the collection, which keeps them consistent.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note can also declare its tags as a property, the way Obsidian does:
  </p>
  <pre><code>---
tags:
  - research
  - ai
---</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both count. A tag written either way appears in the tag list, filters the note list, and nests
    the same. <strong>Front-matter tags used to be ignored entirely</strong> — if your vault
    declares tags this way, they are visible now where they were not before.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tags nest with slashes — <code>#project/website</code>, <code>#project/app</code>. They live in
    the panel’s <strong>Tags</strong> view, which opens on <em>this note’s</em> tags as chips,
    because the usual reason to look at a tag is to find what else shares it. Below that are the
    collection’s tags with how many notes carry each, most-used first — a count of 1 means
    following it leads back here. The forty most-used are listed; type in the field above to find
    any of the rest. Selecting a tag filters the notes in the sidebar. On iPhone the{' '}
    <strong>Tags</strong> tab lists every tag in the collection by name, and choosing one shows its
    notes in Search.
  </p>

  <h2 id="suggestions">What accepted suggestions write</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Suggest Tags</strong>, <strong>Suggest Links</strong> and{' '}
    <strong>Summarise Note</strong> all write to the note’s properties, never into your text:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a tag is added to <code>tags:</code></li>
    <li>a link is added to <code>related:</code> as <code>"[[Note]]"</code> — still a real link, so
        it appears in backlinks and the graph</li>
    <li>a summary is saved to <code>summary:</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each is visible in the panel’s <strong>Properties</strong> view and can be removed there.
    Earlier versions appended tags after your last paragraph, added links under a{' '}
    <code>## Related</code> heading the app created, and pushed summaries in above your first line —
    edits to your prose that you then had to undo by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bookmarks">Bookmarks</h2>
  <p>
    <kbd>⇧⌘D</kbd> — <strong>Note ▸ Add Bookmark</strong> — bookmarks the current note. Bookmarks
    are pinned at the top of the sidebar, under <strong>Bookmarks</strong>, beside{' '}
    <strong>Recents</strong>. They are stored per collection, so each project keeps its own
    shortlist, and the sidebar shows them across every open collection at once.
  </p>

  <h2 id="daily">Daily notes</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Today’s Note</strong> — under the <strong>+</strong> in the bar over the note, or{' '}
    <kbd>⇧⌘T</kbd> — opens (or creates) a note for today. Configure it in{' '}
    <strong>Settings ▸ General</strong>, under <strong>Daily notes</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Folder</strong> — where daily notes live; blank means the collection root.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Date format</strong> — date tokens such as <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>. The settings pane
      shows a worked example of today’s filename as you type.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Quick Capture…</strong> (<kbd>⌃⌘K</kbd>, and under the same <strong>+</strong>) appends
    a thought to today’s note, and <strong>Dictate to Daily Note</strong> (<kbd>⌃⌘D</kbd>) does
    the same by voice. On the Mac, the HelloNotes item in the menu bar captures into today’s note
    without switching apps.
  </p>

  <h2 id="templates">Templates</h2>
  <p>
    Put Markdown files in your templates folder (<strong>Settings ▸ General</strong>, default{' '}
    <code>Templates</code>) and add one to the open note with{' '}
    <strong>Note ▸ Insert Template</strong>, or from the command palette (<kbd>⇧⌘P</kbd>). The
    template is appended to the end of the note, and its date and title tokens are expanded as it
    goes in:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Becomes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;date&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>Today’s date</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;time&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The current time</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>&#123;&#123;title&#125;&#125;</code></td><td>The note’s title</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="files">Folders, moving and attachments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Create a folder with <strong>New Folder…</strong>, under the <strong>+</strong> at the top of
      the sidebar, or <strong>New Folder Here…</strong> in a folder’s own <strong>…</strong> menu,
      and drag notes between folders — the files really move on disk.
    </li>
    <li>Renaming a note rewrites links to it across the collection.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Move to Trash</strong> (<kbd>⌘⌫</kbd>) puts the file in the Trash on the Mac, where
      you can get it back. On iPhone and iPad a folder may have no Trash to move it to, and the
      note is then deleted.
    </li>
    <li>
      Non-Markdown files show in the tree when <strong>View ▸ Show Non-Note Files</strong> is on,
      and open in a native viewer (PDFKit / Quick Look), so a collection can hold its own reference
      material.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ManualLayout>
